'''''Iberodes linifolia''''' ([[synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''Omphalodes linifolia''), also known as '''Venus's navelwort''', '''white-flower navelwort''' and '''petite bourrache''', is an [[annual plant|annual]] [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] [[native plant|native]] to the [[Iberian Peninsula]] and western France,<ref>{{cite web |title=Omphalodes Mill. |url=http://www.floraiberica.es/floraiberica/texto/pdfs/11_138_21_Omphalodes.pdf |publisher=[[Flora Iberica]] |access-date=21 February 2023}}</ref> but has naturalized elsewhere in Europe, North Africa, North America and Chile.<ref>{{cite web |title=Iberodes linifolia (L.) Serrano, R. Carbajal & S. Ortiz |url=https://www.catalogueoflife.org/data/taxon/3P7DX |publisher=[[Catalogue of Life]] |access-date=26 January 2021}}</ref> The plant is small, growing to {{convert|30|-|40|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall by {{convert|15|cm|0|abbr=on}} wide, with blue-green foliage and tiny white or off-white flowers in Spring and Summer. It is distributed throughout dry open areas in full sun.

The Latin [[binomial nomenclature|specific epithet]] ''linifolia'' means “with leaves like flax (''[[Linum]]'')”.<ref name=RHSLG>{{cite book |last=Harrison |first=Lorraine |title=RHS Latin for Gardeners |year=2012 |publisher=Mitchell Beazley |location=United Kingdom |isbn=978-1845337315 }}</ref>

Under the name ''Omphalodes linifolia'', this plant has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web|title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Omphalodes linifolia''|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/11786/Omphalodes-linifolia/Details | access-date = 16 January 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 69
| publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 14 April 2018}}</ref>

